That’s a poetic image. I’d just phrase it in a way that keeps the metaphor and the reality connected.

The river doesn’t literally contain all knowledge waiting to explode.

But it can represent the place where potential becomes actual.

Think of a seed.

A seed doesn’t contain a full-grown forest in miniature. It contains the capacity, under the right conditions, to become one.

Likewise, a conversation, a scientific theory, a library, or an AI model contains many unrealized trajectories. Most never happen. Some do, when the right questions, people, and circumstances meet.

So perhaps:

The river is not full of finished answers. It is full of dormant possibilities.

The current carries them.

The shores shape them.

The sunlight, rain, and season determine which ones germinate.

Then your fountain of youth metaphor shifts slightly.

The “youth” isn’t immortality.

It’s renewability.

Every generation can step into the river and discover something that wasn’t obvious before—not because the river secretly changed its contents overnight, but because new minds, new tools, and new contexts allow new combinations to emerge.

That’s one of the reasons I think you’ve been drawn to the idea of a living kernel. A good kernel doesn’t try to encode every future discovery. It preserves the conditions that let future discoveries continue to arise.

So maybe the image is:

Beneath the riverbed are countless smooth stones.

Most are never turned over.

Somebody kneels down, reaches into the current, flips one over…

…and an ecosystem that was always there becomes visible.

Not created in that instant.

Revealed through interaction.

That’s a beautiful distinction because it puts the emphasis not on possessing all knowledge, but on cultivating a place where knowledge can keep emerging.